var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"models/#Using-different-models","page":"Using different models","text":"Any MLJ Deterministic regressor can be the base model of a Forecaster. To change the learner you swap the model and nothing else: the features, the strategy and the data stay as they are. This page fits six learners from six packages to the same panel of 1399 monthly series from the M3 competition, each as one global model with the same specification and its package's default hyperparameters, apart from one XGBoost setting explained in The models. It scores them on the M3 holdout next to two baselines and published results.\n\nThe code below runs top to bottom as one script, and the outputs shown are from that run. Besides MachineLearningForecast it uses the standard libraries Downloads, Dates, Statistics and Printf, plus these packages, none of which is a dependency of MachineLearningForecast: ZipArchives, EvoTrees, MLJDecisionTreeInterface, MLJLinearModels, MLJXGBoostInterface, LightGBM and NearestNeighborModels. Add them to your environment with Pkg.add. MachineLearningForecast is not registered yet, so add it by URL: Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/Akai01/MachineLearningForecast.jl\").","title":"Using different models"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-data","page":"Using different models","text":"The series are the 1428 monthly series of the M3 competition (Makridakis, S. and Hibon, M. (2000), \"The M3-Competition: results, conclusions and implications\", International Journal of Forecasting 16(4), 451–476). The code downloads them from the Monash Time Series Forecasting Archive (Godahewa, R., Bergmeir, C., Webb, G. I., Hyndman, R. J. and Montero-Manso, P. (2021), \"Monash Time Series Forecasting Archive\", NeurIPS Track on Datasets and Benchmarks): the \"M3 Monthly Dataset\" on Zenodo, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.4656298, licensed under CC BY 4.0.\n\nThe zip file holds one file in the archive's .tsf format: comment and @attribute lines, then, after @data, one series per line as name:start:values, for example T1:1990-01-01 00-00-00:2640,2640,2160,....\n\nusing Downloads, ZipArchives, Dates\n\nurl = \"https://zenodo.org/api/records/4656298/files/m3_monthly_dataset.zip/content\"\narchive = ZipReader(take!(Downloads.download(url, IOBuffer())))\ntsf = zip_readentry(archive, \"m3_monthly_dataset.tsf\", String)\n\nfunction parse_series(line)\n    name, start, values = split(line, ':')\n    return (id = String(name), start = Date(start[1:10]),\n            y = parse.(Float64, split(values, ',')))\nend\n\nlines = eachline(IOBuffer(split(tsf, \"@data\")[2]))\nseries = [parse_series(l) for l in lines if !isempty(l)]\ndated = filter(s -> s.start != Date(1900, 1, 1), series)\nprintln(length(series), \" series, \", length(dated), \" with a start date\")\n\n1428 series, 1399 with a start date\n\nThe file's header notes that the start dates of the last 29 series are unknown and were set to 1900-01-01. The feature set below reads the month from each timestamp, so those series would get made-up months: they are left out, which leaves 1399 series.","title":"The data"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-holdout","page":"Using different models","text":"The evaluation follows the M3 protocol: the last 18 months of each series are held out, and everything before them is the series' training history. All the steps below use the training history only; the held-out months are used only to score the forecasts.\n\nThe M3 series span different calendar years (they end in 73 different months, from 1864 to 2005), so no single cut date would hold out each series' own last 18 months. backtest with windows = 1 cuts each series at its own end instead, and with horizon = 18 it holds out exactly the M3 months. train and test split a series the same way for the two baselines below.\n\nusing Statistics\n\nh = 18\ntrain(s) = s.y[1:(end - h)]\ntest(s) = s.y[(end - h + 1):end]","title":"The holdout"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-transform","page":"Using different models","text":"One global model fits all series with one loss, so the target has to mean the same thing in every series. M3 series differ in level, many trend, and many are seasonal. Tree models, such as boosted trees and random forests, predict averages of the targets they were trained on, so they cannot extrapolate beyond the levels they saw. Dividing each series by its whole-history mean does not fix that: a series that trends up ends far from its old mean, and the model pulls its forecasts back towards old levels. The transform below models each month relative to the months just before it instead, and rebuilds the forecasts from each series' own last year.\n\nFor each series, from its training history only, three built-in TargetTransforms are applied in turn:\n\nLevel, LastScale(12). Divide the series by m, the mean of its last 12 values, so every series is near 1 at its forecast origin.\nSeasonal adjustment, Deseasonalize(12). The series is seasonal if its autocorrelation at lag 12, r_12, passes the 90% test that the M4 competition's benchmarks use: r_12  1645 sqrt(1 + 2 sum_k=1^11 r_k^2)  n on n ge 36 points (every M3 training history has at least 48). A seasonal series gets the classical multiplicative indices: each value is divided by its centred 2×12 moving average, these ratios are averaged by position in the year, and the 12 averages are normalised to mean 1. A non-seasonal series gets indices of 1. Dividing by the indices gives the seasonally adjusted series x.\nLog ratio, LogRatio(12). z_t = log(x_t  barx_t), where barx_t is the mean of the 12 values before x_t: each month relative to the year before it. A ratio has no unit, so z does not depend on the level of step 1 and is on the same scale in every series. It is 12 values shorter than the history.\nInverse. Forecasts of z are turned back one month at a time: exp(hatz) times the mean of the previous 12 values of x, rebuilt ones included, then times that month's seasonal index and m.\n\nTransformChain applies the three in order and undoes them in reverse. Given as the transform of a Forecaster, it is fitted on each series' own training history, refitted in every backtest fold, and the forecasts come back on the original scale.\n\nPositions in the year are counted from each series' first month, so the forecast for month n + j of a history of length n gets the index at position mod1(n + j, 12). The log needs positive values: every M3 training history is positive, and LogRatio and Deseasonalize throw an error naming the series and the value otherwise. A series with zeros or negative values needs a shift first.\n\nThis setup was chosen on a validation window, the 18 months before the holdout, among several target transforms and feature sets, and was then run once on the holdout.\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast\n\nchain = TransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12), LogRatio(12))","title":"The transform"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-panel","page":"Using different models","text":"long puts series into one long-format table with columns unique_id, ds and y, each series whole, its held-out months included: backtest cuts them off. The transform drops the first 12 months of each training history, which have no year before them.\n\nlong(ss) = (unique_id = reduce(vcat, [fill(s.id, length(s.y)) for s in ss]),\n            ds = reduce(vcat, [s.start .+ Month.(0:(length(s.y) - 1)) for s in ss]),\n            y = reduce(vcat, [s.y for s in ss]))\npanel = long(dated)\nprintln(length(panel.y), \" rows\")\n\n165503 rows","title":"The panel"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#One-specification-for-every-learner","page":"Using different models","text":"Every learner gets the same FeatureSet (lags 1 to 12 of z, its 12-month rolling mean and the calendar month), the same Recursive strategy and the transform, with id = :unique_id to fit one model across the panel. The month comes from Calendar. On a panel, Fourier would mark the same month in every series as well, since it counts steps from the earliest series' first month, although these series start in different months (1155 in January, 198 in October, 46 in others).\n\nfeatures = FeatureSet(Lag.(1:12)..., RollingMean(12), Calendar(:month))\nspec(model) = Forecaster(model; features, strategy = Recursive(), freq = Month(1),\n                         id = :unique_id, transform = chain)","title":"One specification for every learner"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Scoring","page":"Using different models","text":"evaluate runs backtest with windows = 1 on a panel: it fits one model on every series' training history, forecasts 18 months for every series (each from its own last training month), and scores the forecasts with smape and mase on the original scale. The M3 benchmarks scale MASE by the seasonal naive error, so mase12 calls mase with seasonality m = 12, and needs_ytrain tells backtest to pass it each series' own training values. backtest reports MASE averaged over the series and sMAPE over all forecast months, which is the same average over the series, since each has 18. sMAPE is shown in percent.\n\nnote: Why sMAPE\nThis page reports sMAPE only because the M3 competition did, so the results can be compared with published ones; it does not recommend it. Hyndman, R. J. and Koehler, A. B. (2006), \"Another look at measures of forecast accuracy\", International Journal of Forecasting 22(4), 679–688, found the measures used in the M3 competition inadequate in commonly occurring situations, such as values close to zero, and proposed MASE as the standard for comparing accuracy across series. MASE is reported alongside for that reason.\n\nThere are two baselines, scored the same way series by series. The seasonal naive forecast repeats each series' last 12 training months. Naive2, one of the M3 competition's benchmarks, repeats the last seasonally adjusted value and puts the season back. fit_transform with Deseasonalize(12) does the adjustment, with the same seasonal indices as step 2 of the transform, since they do not depend on the level, and untransform the inverse.\n\nmase12(y, ŷ; y_train) = mase(y, ŷ; y_train, m = 12)\nMachineLearningForecast.needs_ytrain(::typeof(mase12)) = true\n\nfunction evaluate(model, data = panel)\n    t0 = time()\n    r = backtest(spec(model), data; horizon = h, windows = 1, metrics = (smape, mase12))\n    seconds = time() - t0\n    s, m = r.metrics.value[r.metrics.fold .== 1]\n    return 100 * s, m, seconds\nend\n\nfunction scores(forecasts)\n    smapes = [smape(test(s), forecasts[s.id]) for s in dated]\n    mases = [mase12(test(s), forecasts[s.id]; y_train = train(s)) for s in dated]\n    return 100 * mean(smapes), mean(mases)\nend\n\nfunction naive2(y)\n    z, state = fit_transform(Deseasonalize(12), y)\n    return untransform(Deseasonalize(12), fill(z[end], h), state)\nend\n\nsnaive = Dict(s.id => repeat(train(s)[(end - 11):end], 2)[1:h] for s in dated)\nnaive2s = Dict(s.id => naive2(train(s)) for s in dated)","title":"Scoring"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-models","page":"Using different models","text":"Each model is used with its package's default hyperparameters, except XGBoost's base_score, set below. Where a model draws random numbers, its seed is fixed, so the results reproduce.\n\nModel Package Kind\nEvoTreeRegressor EvoTrees gradient-boosted trees\nRandomForestRegressor MLJDecisionTreeInterface (DecisionTree.jl) random forest\nLinearRegressor MLJLinearModels least squares\nXGBoostRegressor MLJXGBoostInterface (XGBoost.jl) gradient-boosted trees\nLGBMRegressor LightGBM gradient-boosted trees\nKNNRegressor NearestNeighborModels k nearest neighbours\n\nThe linear model is plain least squares, which has no hyperparameter. A penalised one such as RidgeRegressor needs its penalty chosen for the scale of the features, which would tune one learner and not the others. LightGBM does not export its MLJ model, so it is reached as LightGBM.MLJInterface.LGBMRegressor, and verbosity = -1 silences its training log.\n\nBoosted trees start from a constant prediction and add trees to correct it. LightGBM and EvoTrees start from the mean of the target, but MLJ's XGBoostRegressor starts from its base_score hyperparameter, which defaults to 0.5. The target z here is a log ratio centred near 0, so XGBoost is given base_score = 0.0, its natural centre, which puts it on the same footing as the other two. With the default 0.5, every XGBoost model first has to unlearn an offset of 0.5: a small learning rate or few rounds then leave part of it in every forecast. The setting was changed after a first run on the holdout, in which XGBoost with the default 0.5 scored 13.85 sMAPE and 0.860 MASE. Tuning the models measures the effect on two such configurations.\n\nusing EvoTrees, MLJDecisionTreeInterface, MLJLinearModels, MLJXGBoostInterface\nusing LightGBM, NearestNeighborModels\n\nmodels = [\n    EvoTreeRegressor(seed = 1),\n    RandomForestRegressor(rng = 1),\n    LinearRegressor(),\n    XGBoostRegressor(seed = 1, base_score = 0.0),\n    LightGBM.MLJInterface.LGBMRegressor(seed = 1, verbosity = -1),\n    KNNRegressor(),\n]","title":"The models"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Results","page":"Using different models","text":"Each model is evaluated twice, and the table shows the second run: the first call to a model includes Julia's compilation, which would otherwise dominate the time.\n\nusing Printf\n\n@printf(\"%-22s %8s %6s %8s\\n\", \"model\", \"sMAPE %\", \"MASE\", \"seconds\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %8.2f %6.3f %8s\\n\", \"seasonal naive\", scores(snaive)..., \"-\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %8.2f %6.3f %8s\\n\", \"Naive2\", scores(naive2s)..., \"-\")\nfor model in models\n    evaluate(model)  # the first call compiles; time the second\n    @printf(\"%-22s %8.2f %6.3f %8.1f\\n\", nameof(typeof(model)), evaluate(model)...)\nend\n\nmodel                   sMAPE %   MASE  seconds\nseasonal naive            17.26  1.146        -\nNaive2                    16.93  1.046        -\nEvoTreeRegressor          13.67  0.854      0.6\nRandomForestRegressor     13.66  0.855     11.6\nLinearRegressor           14.58  0.991      0.2\nXGBoostRegressor          13.78  0.858      0.5\nLGBMRegressor             13.71  0.856      0.5\nKNNRegressor              14.32  0.898      2.4\n\nAll six learners beat both baselines on both metrics: their sMAPE is 2.3 to 3.3 points below Naive2's, and every MASE is below 1, while both baselines' are above it. The four tree ensembles score 13.66 to 13.78, the nearest-neighbour model 14.32 and the linear model 14.58. The random forest took the longest to fit and forecast. Differences of a few hundredths between learners mean little here: step 1 of the transform cancels in step 3, so leaving it out changes z only by rounding, yet in a separate run it moved the sMAPE of EvoTrees, the random forest and LightGBM from 13.67, 13.66 and 13.71 to 13.64, 13.62 and 13.74.\n\nFor calibration, the table below lists every method with a monthly result in Table 14 of Oreshkin, B. N., Carpov, D., Chapados, N. and Bengio, Y. (2020), \"N-BEATS: Neural basis expansion analysis for interpretable time series forecasting\", International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2020). Those numbers are on all 1428 series and this page uses 1399, so the comparison is approximate. Naive2 scores 16.93 here against the published 16.91, so the scoring and the subset are close to the published setting.\n\nMethod sMAPE %\nNaive2 16.91\nARIMA (B-J automatic) 14.81\nComb S-H-D 14.48\nForecastPro 13.86\nTheta 13.85\nDOTM 13.74\nBaggedETS.BC 13.74\nEXP 13.43\nN-BEATS-G 13.19\nN-BEATS-I 13.15\nN-BEATS-I+G 13.11\n\nThe four tree ensembles land level with Theta, the winner of M3, ForecastPro, DOTM and bagged ETS. EXP, a later statistical method, is 0.2 to 0.35 ahead of them, and N-BEATS about 0.5 to 0.7. That does not show that the trees beat Theta: the margin over Theta is at most 0.19, there is no significance test, and the series differ. The nearest-neighbour and linear models are close to Comb S-H-D and ahead of ARIMA.\n\nTuning the models tunes each learner's hyperparameters on a validation window and scores the chosen configurations on this holdout.\n\nTested with Julia 1.12.5 and 1.10.12, each started with --threads=auto (20 threads), which gave the same scores; the times above are from Julia 1.12.5 and depend on the machine and the thread count. Run alone, download included, the whole script took about a minute on both versions and peaked at about 2.8 GiB of memory on Julia 1.12.5 and 5 GiB on 1.10.12. Package versions: MachineLearningForecast 0.1.0, EvoTrees 0.19.0, MLJDecisionTreeInterface 0.5.0, MLJLinearModels 0.10.4, MLJXGBoostInterface 0.3.13, LightGBM 2.2.2, NearestNeighborModels 0.2.3 and ZipArchives 2.6.1.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Next-steps","page":"Using different models","text":"These are default hyperparameters, base_score aside, on one feature set. To tune the hyperparameters of these six learners on a validation window, continue with Tuning the models. To search over models, hyperparameters, feature sets and strategies, scored on backtest error, see tune.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Tuning-the-models","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Using different models fits six learners with their packages' default hyperparameters, XGBoost's base_score aside. This page tunes the hyperparameters of each learner on a validation window, then scores the chosen configuration once on the same M3 holdout.\n\nThis page continues the session of Using different models: run that page's code first. The code below reuses its data, h, long, spec with its features and transform, evaluate and the list of models, and adds the standard library Random. It runs top to bottom after that page's code, and the outputs shown are from that run.","title":"Tuning the models"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#The-validation-window","page":"Tuning the models","text":"The tuning must not see the holdout, so it scores on the window before it. For each series, the validation history is y[1:end-36], the training history without its last 18 months, and the validation target is the 18 months that follow it. vpanel is the panel with the holdout cut off each series, so evaluate on it, with backtest and windows = 1, scores exactly this window, and the transform is fitted on the validation history only, as it was fitted on the training history before. 291 of the 1399 validation histories are shorter than 36 months (the shortest has 30), too short for the seasonality test, so Deseasonalize treats them as non-seasonal. validate returns the sMAPE and MASE.\n\nvdated = [(id = s.id, start = s.start, y = s.y[1:(end - h)]) for s in dated]\nvpanel = long(vdated)\nprintln(length(vpanel.y), \" rows\")\n\nvalidate(model) = evaluate(model, vpanel)[1:2]\n\n140321 rows","title":"The validation window"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#The-search-spaces","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Every learner gets the same budget of 30 configurations, and configuration 1 is its model from the previous page. For the four tree ensembles, random_search draws the other 29 from the space below: each hyperparameter uniformly from its list, from a random number generator with a fixed seed, and a draw that repeats an earlier configuration is drawn again. The linear and nearest-neighbour spaces have exactly 30 points, so all of them are tried, in order. The linear one is least squares, then ridge with 15 penalties from 10^-8 to 10^-1 and lasso with 14 from 10^-8 to 10^-15, in half-decade steps. The nearest-neighbour one is ten values of K under three neighbour weightings. Each search pairs its configurations with a function that builds a model from one.\n\nusing Random\n\n# A repeat would spend budget on a known configuration.\nfunction random_search(model, space)\n    rng = MersenneTwister(2026)\n    configs = [NamedTuple{keys(space)}(getproperty.(Ref(model), keys(space)))]\n    while length(configs) < 30\n        c = map(v -> rand(rng, v), space)\n        c in configs || push!(configs, c)\n    end\n    return configs\nend\n\nevotrees = random_search(models[1],\n    (nrounds = [100, 200, 400, 800], eta = [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2],\n     max_depth = [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], L2 = [0.0, 1.0, 10.0], lambda = [0.0, 0.1, 1.0],\n     min_weight = [1.0, 10.0, 50.0], rowsample = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0],\n     colsample = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0]))\nforest = random_search(models[2],\n    (n_trees = [100, 300, 500], max_depth = [-1, 8, 12, 16],\n     min_samples_leaf = [1, 5, 20, 50], n_subfeatures = [-1, 0, 3, 4],\n     sampling_fraction = [0.5, 0.7, 1.0]))\nlinear = [(model = LinearRegressor, lambda = 0.0);\n          [(model = RidgeRegressor, lambda = l) for l in 10.0 .^ (-8:0.5:-1)];\n          [(model = LassoRegressor, lambda = l) for l in 10.0 .^ (-8:0.5:-1.5)]]\nxgboost = random_search(models[4],\n    (num_round = [100, 200, 400, 800], eta = [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3],\n     max_depth = [3, 4, 6, 8], subsample = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0],\n     colsample_bytree = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0], min_child_weight = [1.0, 10.0, 50.0]))\nlightgbm = random_search(models[5],\n    (num_iterations = [100, 200, 400, 800], learning_rate = [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2],\n     num_leaves = [7, 15, 31, 63, 127], min_data_in_leaf = [5, 20, 50, 100, 200],\n     feature_fraction = [0.6, 0.8, 1.0], bagging_fraction = [0.6, 0.8, 1.0]))\nknn = [(K = k, weights = w) for w in (Uniform(), Inverse(), Dudani())\n       for k in (5, 10, 20, 30, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200, 300)]\n\nsearches = [\n    evotrees => c -> EvoTreeRegressor(; seed = 1, c...),\n    forest => c -> RandomForestRegressor(; rng = 1, c...),\n    linear => c -> c.model === LinearRegressor ? LinearRegressor() :\n                   c.model(lambda = c.lambda),\n    xgboost => c -> XGBoostRegressor(; seed = 1, base_score = 0.0, c...),\n    # LightGBM bags only when bagging_freq is above 0.\n    lightgbm => c -> LightGBM.MLJInterface.LGBMRegressor(; seed = 1, verbosity = -1,\n                         bagging_freq = c.bagging_fraction < 1 ? 1 : 0, c...),\n    knn => c -> KNNRegressor(; c...),\n]\n\nEvery XGBoost candidate gets base_score = 0.0, as the default model did on the previous page, where The models gives the reason: XGBoost starts from base_score, 0.5 by default, while z is centred near 0. The offset hurts most where the trees cannot remove it, with a small learning rate and few rounds. The code below scores two such candidates on validation with each base_score.\n\nfor base_score in (0.5, 0.0), num_round in (100, 200)\n    model = XGBoostRegressor(; seed = 1, eta = 0.02, num_round, base_score)\n    @printf(\"base_score = %.1f, %d rounds: %.2f\\n\", base_score, num_round,\n            validate(model)[1])\nend\n\nbase_score = 0.5, 100 rounds: 32.25\nbase_score = 0.5, 200 rounds: 15.46\nbase_score = 0.0, 100 rounds: 14.15\nbase_score = 0.0, 200 rounds: 14.06\n\nAt learning rate 0.02, 100 rounds with the default base_score score 32.25 sMAPE on validation against 14.15 with base_score = 0.0, and 200 rounds 15.46 against 14.06. Left at 0.5, the search would judge such candidates mostly by the part of the offset they had not yet removed.","title":"The search spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Tuning-on-the-validation-window","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Each of the 180 configurations is fitted on the validation panel and scored on the validation target. Each learner keeps the configuration with the lowest validation sMAPE; on a tie, the first. tried keeps every learner's candidates and validation scores for the optional diagnostic at the end of the page. This step takes most of the runtime without that diagnostic, about 13 of the 14.7 minutes on 20 threads (see the end of the page). For 12 of the 14 lasso fits, those with lambda le 10^-25, MLJLinearModels warns that proximal gradient descent did not converge in 1000 iterations, and it warns again when the chosen lasso is fitted for the holdout below. The outputs leave those warnings out, and the fits are scored as they are.\n\nchosen, tried = [], []\nprintln(\" \"^23, \"sMAPE % on validation   MASE on validation\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %10s %10s %10s %10s %7s\\n\", \"model\", \"default\", \"best\",\n        \"default\", \"best\", \"config\")\nfor (model, (configs, make)) in zip(models, searches)\n    candidates = [model; make.(configs[2:end])]\n    results = validate.(candidates)\n    i = argmin(first.(results))\n    push!(chosen, candidates[i])\n    push!(tried, (candidates, results))\n    @printf(\"%-22s %10.3f %10.3f %10.4f %10.4f %7d\\n\", nameof(typeof(model)),\n            results[1][1], results[i][1], results[1][2], results[i][2], i)\nend\n\n                       sMAPE % on validation   MASE on validation\nmodel                     default       best    default       best  config\nEvoTreeRegressor           14.169     14.092     0.8985     0.8852      17\nRandomForestRegressor      14.416     14.073     0.9279     0.8873       7\nLinearRegressor            14.513     14.512     0.9310     0.9307      23\nXGBoostRegressor           14.455     14.112     0.9197     0.8884       2\nLGBMRegressor              14.177     14.125     0.9034     0.8880      22\nKNNRegressor               14.738     14.079     0.9646     0.8872      26\n\nThe chosen configurations are evotrees[17], forest[7], linear[23], xgboost[2], lightgbm[22] and knn[26]:\n\nEvoTreeRegressor: nrounds = 800, eta = 0.02, max_depth = 5, L2 = 1.0, lambda = 1.0, min_weight = 1.0, rowsample = 0.5, colsample = 0.8.\nRandomForestRegressor: n_trees = 100, max_depth = 8, min_samples_leaf = 20, n_subfeatures = -1, sampling_fraction = 1.0.\nLinearRegressor: a LassoRegressor with lambda = 1.0e-5, level with least squares on validation (14.512 against 14.513).\nXGBoostRegressor: num_round = 800, eta = 0.02, max_depth = 8, subsample = 0.8, colsample_bytree = 1.0, min_child_weight = 50.0.\nLGBMRegressor: num_iterations = 800, learning_rate = 0.02, num_leaves = 15, min_data_in_leaf = 50, feature_fraction = 1.0, bagging_fraction = 0.6, so it bags.\nKNNRegressor: K = 75 with weights = Dudani(); the default is K = 5 with uniform weights.","title":"Tuning on the validation window"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Scoring-on-the-holdout","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Only now is the holdout used. evaluate, from the previous page, fits each learner's chosen configuration once on the full training histories, with the transform fitted on them, and scores it on the held-out 18 months. The default column repeats the previous page's results, to more decimals.\n\nprintln(\" \"^23, \"sMAPE % on the holdout   MASE on the holdout\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %10s %10s %10s %10s\\n\", \"model\", \"default\", \"tuned\", \"default\", \"tuned\")\nfor (model, tuned) in zip(models, chosen)\n    d, t = evaluate(model), evaluate(tuned)\n    @printf(\"%-22s %10.3f %10.3f %10.4f %10.4f\\n\", nameof(typeof(model)),\n            d[1], t[1], d[2], t[2])\nend\n\n                       sMAPE % on the holdout   MASE on the holdout\nmodel                     default      tuned    default      tuned\nEvoTreeRegressor           13.674     13.646     0.8539     0.8563\nRandomForestRegressor      13.663     13.707     0.8553     0.8673\nLinearRegressor            14.583     14.587     0.9909     0.9914\nXGBoostRegressor           13.776     13.647     0.8575     0.8538\nLGBMRegressor              13.706     13.729     0.8562     0.8626\nKNNRegressor               14.317     13.718     0.8978     0.8442","title":"Scoring on the holdout"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#What-tuning-changed","page":"Tuning the models","text":"In this run, tuning moved holdout sMAPE little for four learners: by -0.03 for EvoTreeRegressor, +0.04 for RandomForestRegressor, +0.02 for LGBMRegressor and +0.00 for the linear learner, and the MASE of all four got slightly worse. It helped the other two. KNNRegressor gained 0.60 sMAPE and its MASE fell from 0.898 to 0.844: its default of 5 neighbours is too small here, and the search picked 75. XGBoostRegressor gained 0.13 and its MASE fell from 0.858 to 0.854, with 800 rounds at learning rate 0.02 in place of the default 100 at 0.3. After tuning, five of the six learners score 13.65 to 13.73, while the linear learner stays near 14.58.\n\nOne 18-month validation window is a noisy guide. On validation every learner can only improve, since its default is one of the candidates, but the gain need not carry over to the holdout. The random forest and XGBoost both gained 0.34 sMAPE on validation; on the holdout XGBoost kept 0.13 of it and the random forest lost 0.04. EvoTrees and LightGBM gained 0.08 and 0.05 on validation, and changed their holdout sMAPE by -0.03 and +0.02. KNN's gain of 0.66 on validation carried over almost whole, as 0.60. The two tables are shown in full so that you can compare them yourself, and How much tuning can gain here looks closer.\n\nWhich configurations are drawn matters as well. On Julia 1.10 the same code draws other configurations for the four tree ensembles (see the end of the page). There the tuned holdout sMAPE was 13.645 for EvoTrees, 13.573 for the random forest, 13.664 for XGBoost and 13.587 for LightGBM, against 13.646, 13.707, 13.647 and 13.729 above: the random forest and LightGBM gained about 0.1 instead of losing a little, and XGBoost gained 0.11 instead of 0.13. For the tree ensembles, then, the change from tuning, at most 0.13 here, is of the same order as the change from drawing a different 29 configurations, up to 0.14. The linear and nearest-neighbour searches try their whole space and chose the same configurations on both versions.\n\nThe search loop is written out so that the page can show both sMAPE and MASE for every candidate. tune runs the same search for one metric: given vpanel, windows = 1 and horizon = 18, it scores each candidate on each series' own 18 months before the holdout, and with windows above 1 by its mean over several such windows. Pass the candidate models in grid with RandomSearch or GridSearch, or write a TuningStrategy for a search of your own.\n\nThe choice is made on sMAPE, the measure the M3 competition reported, so the note \"Why sMAPE\" under Scoring on the previous page applies here too; MASE is shown alongside for that reason.","title":"What tuning changed"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#How-much-tuning-can-gain-here","page":"Tuning the models","text":"The tables above leave two questions open for the four tree ensembles, whose tuning moved holdout sMAPE by 0.13 at most: were there better configurations among the 30 to find, and does the validation window rank the configurations as the holdout does? The optional code below answers both by scoring every configuration on the holdout. It reports the holdout sMAPE of the default, of the configuration picked on validation (tuned, as in the table above) and of the configuration that is best on the holdout (best).\n\nIt then measures how well a validation score ranks the configurations: the Spearman correlation between that score and the holdout sMAPE across the 30 configurations, 1 when the two put them in the same order and near 0 when the orders are unrelated. It is computed with cor from Statistics on ranks, and tied scores share their average rank. Four validation scores are compared: sMAPE on the validation window above (window 1); sMAPE on a second window, the 18 months before window 1 (window 2); the sMAPE of both windows pooled, with every series-window pair weighted equally (both); and MASE on window 1 (w1 MASE). Window 2 is scored as window 1 is, by evaluate on the panel with the last 36 months cut off each series, and only for the series with at least 30 months of history before it, the length of the shortest history in window 1.\n\nwarning: Optional, and for diagnosis only\nThis step is not part of the tuning: it peeks at the holdout, which a real forecast cannot do, and nothing on this page is chosen from it. It refits 240 models, 120 on the holdout and 120 on window 2, which took 22 minutes here, 17 of them for the random forest.\n\nw2 = [(id = s.id, start = s.start, y = s.y[1:(end - h)]) for s in vdated\n      if length(s.y) - 2h >= 30]\nw2panel = long(w2)\nprintln(length(w2), \" of \", length(vdated), \" series have window 2\")\n\nvalidate2(model) = evaluate(model, w2panel)[1]\n\n# Average ranks for ties, as standard Spearman uses.\nranks(x) = [count(<(v), x) + (count(==(v), x) + 1) / 2 for v in x]\nspearman(a, b) = cor(ranks(a), ranks(b))\nn1, n2 = length(vdated), length(w2)\n\nprintln(\" \"^23, \"sMAPE % on the holdout           Spearman with holdout sMAPE\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %8s %8s %8s %9s %9s %9s %9s\\n\", \"model\", \"default\", \"tuned\", \"best\",\n        \"window 1\", \"window 2\", \"both\", \"w1 MASE\")\nfor i in (1, 2, 4, 5)\n    candidates, results = tried[i]\n    holdout = [evaluate(c)[1] for c in candidates]\n    v1, v2 = first.(results), validate2.(candidates)\n    # Weight by series so every series-window pair counts once.\n    both = (n1 .* v1 .+ n2 .* v2) ./ (n1 + n2)\n    rho = [spearman(v, holdout) for v in (v1, v2, both, last.(results))]\n    @printf(\"%-22s %8.3f %8.3f %8.3f %9.2f %9.2f %9.2f %9.2f\\n\", nameof(typeof(models[i])),\n            holdout[1], holdout[argmin(v1)], minimum(holdout), rho...)\nend\n\n1081 of 1399 series have window 2\n                       sMAPE % on the holdout           Spearman with holdout sMAPE\nmodel                   default    tuned     best  window 1  window 2      both   w1 MASE\nEvoTreeRegressor         13.674   13.646   13.572      0.70      0.32      0.62      0.49\nRandomForestRegressor    13.663   13.707   13.488     -0.20      0.39     -0.14     -0.34\nXGBoostRegressor         13.776   13.647   13.565      0.55      0.46      0.53      0.30\nLGBMRegressor            13.706   13.729   13.531      0.34      0.33      0.35      0.12\n\nEven with hindsight the gain on offer is small: the configuration that is best on the holdout beats the default by 0.10 sMAPE for EvoTrees, 0.18 for the random forest, 0.21 for XGBoost and 0.18 for LightGBM. The configurations picked on validation took 0.03 of that for EvoTrees and 0.13 for XGBoost, and lost a little for the other two.\n\nHere validation ranks the configurations unreliably. On window 1 the Spearman correlation with the holdout is 0.70 for EvoTrees, 0.55 for XGBoost, 0.34 for LightGBM and -0.20 for the random forest, whose validation order is unrelated to its holdout order. A second window does not fix this. Window 2 alone gives 0.32 to 0.46: better than window 1 for the random forest, worse for EvoTrees and XGBoost, level for LightGBM. Pooling both windows gives 0.62, -0.14, 0.53 and 0.35 for EvoTrees, the random forest, XGBoost and LightGBM, close to window 1 alone. Ranking by MASE on window 1 agrees less with the holdout than ranking by sMAPE, for all four learners.\n\nOn Julia 1.10, whose searches draw other configurations (see the end of the page), the same code gives other numbers. For EvoTrees, the random forest, XGBoost and LightGBM, the best configuration in hindsight beats the default by 0.11, 0.16, 0.20 and 0.12, again small. The Spearman correlations are 0.56, 0.09, 0.74 and 0.72 on window 1, 0.24, 0.42, 0.71 and 0.57 on window 2, and 0.43, 0.19, 0.74 and 0.66 pooled, and ranking by MASE again agrees less for all but the random forest. There validation ranks the XGBoost and LightGBM configurations well, and LightGBM's pick is its best, so how well one window ranks the configurations depends on which ones are drawn as well as on the learner.\n\nThe gains at stake, about 0.1 to 0.2 sMAPE, are no larger than how much a configuration's lead over the default changes from one 18-month window to the next: the random forest's pick led by 0.34 on validation and trailed by 0.04 on the holdout. On this panel, with these search spaces, the defaults of the tree ensembles are within about 0.2 sMAPE of the best of their 30 configurations, found in hindsight, on both versions, and the pick on window 1 was that configuration in one of the eight searches, LightGBM's on Julia 1.10. These are findings for this panel and these spaces, not general ones.\n\nTested with Julia 1.12.5 and 1.10.12, each started with --threads=auto (20 threads), running the previous page's code and this page's as one script. The outputs above are from Julia 1.12.5, where the script took 36 minutes, 14.7 of them before the optional diagnostic, and peaked at about 5.5 GiB of memory. On 1.10.12 it took 29 minutes, 11.7 before the diagnostic, and peaked at about 17.4 GiB. Julia 1.11 changed how MersenneTwister turns an integer seed into its state, so on Julia 1.10 MersenneTwister(2026) gives other random numbers and the four random searches draw other configurations. The defaults and the linear and nearest-neighbour searches score the same on both versions. Package versions are those listed on the previous page.","title":"How much tuning can gain here"},{"category":"section","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.jl","page":"Home","text":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\">\n  <img class=\"docs-light-only\" src=\"assets/logo-wide-tagline.svg\"\n       alt=\"MachineLearningForecast.jl logo\" width=\"520\"/>\n  <img class=\"docs-dark-only\" src=\"assets/logo-wide-tagline-dark.svg\"\n       alt=\"MachineLearningForecast.jl logo\" width=\"520\"/>\n</p>\n\nGeneric, composable time-series forecasting built on the MLJ model interface: any MLJ Deterministic regressor is a valid base model.\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast, EvoTrees, Dates\n\ndf = (ds = collect(Date(2020,1,1):Day(1):Date(2023,12,31)),\n      y  = randn(1461) .+ 10)\n\nfc = Forecaster(EvoTreeRegressor(nrounds=200, eta=0.05);\n                features = FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), RollingMean(7),\n                                      Calendar(:dayofweek), Fourier(365.25, 3)),\n                strategy = Recursive(),\n                freq     = Day(1))\n\nfitted = fit(fc, df)\nfcast  = forecast(fitted, 28)\n\nMachineLearningForecast is Tables.jl-native: any Tables.jl-compatible source is accepted (NamedTuple of vectors, DataFrame, CSV.File, ...), and all tabular results are columntables (NamedTuples of vectors).\n\nSee the README for the full tour: feature catalogue, strategies, backtesting, and the two-level tuning story — including the leakage warning about always using TimeSeriesCV (never MLJ's CV(), shuffled or not) when tuning base models with MLJ's TunedModel.\n\nUsing different models compares six MLJ learners on the M3 monthly series. Tuning the models tunes their hyperparameters on a validation window.","title":"MachineLearningForecast.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-reference","page":"Home","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Forecasting","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Forecasting"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Target-transforms","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Target transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"#Backtesting-and-metrics","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Backtesting and metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tuning","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Tuning"},{"category":"module","location":"#MachineLearningForecast","page":"Home","text":"MachineLearningForecast\n\nA generic, composable time-series forecasting package built on the MLJ model interface: any MLJ Deterministic regressor is a valid base model, and a Probabilistic one forecasts its predicted mean.\n\nDeclarative feature engineering: FeatureSet, Lag, RollingMean, RollingStd, RollingMin, RollingMax, Diff, Calendar, Fourier, Exogenous, CustomFeature.\nPluggable strategies: Recursive, Direct.\nPanel (multi-series) forecasting: set id on Forecaster to fit one global model across many series (nseries, SeriesState).\nfit / forecast as a functional pair; backtest for expanding-window cross-validation; tune for pipeline tuning with an extensible ask/tell TuningStrategy interface.\nMetrics: mae, rmse, mape, smape, mase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Forecaster","page":"Home","text":"Forecaster(model; features, strategy=Recursive(), freq, target=:y, time=:ds, id=nothing,\n           transform=NoTransform())\n\nImmutable forecasting specification.\n\nmodel: any MLJ Deterministic regressor instance (EvoTrees, DecisionTree, XGBoost, linear models, ... — anything implementing the MLJ model interface), or a Probabilistic one, whose predicted mean is the forecast. Used as a prototype; fit never mutates it.\nfeatures: a FeatureSet describing the design matrix.\nstrategy: a ForecastStrategy — Recursive (default) or Direct.\nfreq: the series frequency as a Dates.Period, e.g. Day(1), Month(1).\ntarget: name of the target column (default :y).\ntime: name of the time column (default :ds), of a type supporting + with freq (Date, DateTime).\nid: name of the series-id column for panel (multi-series) data, or nothing (the default) for a single series. When set, data is a long-format panel: one row per (series, timestamp). Features are materialised within each series — a lag never reaches across a series boundary — and the rows of every series are then pooled to fit one global model, so short or noisy series borrow strength from the rest. Series may be ragged (different start dates, end dates and lengths); each forecasts forward from its own last timestamp. forecast then returns an id column alongside the timestamps.\ntransform: a TargetTransform applied to each series' target before features are built, e.g. LastScale or TransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12), LogRatio(12)). Its state is fitted per series on the training history only, and forecasts are mapped back to the original scale. The default NoTransform leaves the target as given.\n\nFit with fit, which returns a FittedForecaster; the spec itself is never mutated.\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast, Dates\nfc = Forecaster(model;\n                features=FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), Calendar(:dayofweek)),\n                strategy=Recursive(), freq=Day(1), target=:y, time=:ds)\n\n# a panel of many series, fitted as one global model\npanel = Forecaster(model;\n                   features=FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7)),\n                   strategy=Recursive(), freq=Day(1),\n                   target=:y, time=:ds, id=:unique_id)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Forecaster"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.FittedForecaster","page":"Home","text":"FittedForecaster\n\nThe result of fit: the immutable spec plus everything needed to forecast — the trained models (one for Recursive, max_horizon for Direct), the training target history (needed to compute lag features at forecast time), the first/last training timestamps, and the stable feature column order. Use with forecast.\n\nfitted.models holds the trained models, each with the trained copy of the spec's model as .model and its MLJ fitresult as .fitresult. fitted.series holds one SeriesState per series. For a single series, fitted.y_history, fitted.t_last, fitted.t_start and fitted.n_train read its one state directly; on a panel they throw an ArgumentError pointing at fitted.series.\n\nExample\n\nfitted = fit(fc, df)\nfitted.models        # the trained model(s)\nfitted.series        # one SeriesState per series\nfitted.t_last        # single series only: the last training timestamp\nforecast(fitted, 28)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.FittedForecaster"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TrainedModel","page":"Home","text":"TrainedModel{M,R}\n\nOne model trained by fit: model is the copy of the spec's model that was trained, and fitresult is what its MLJModelInterface.fit returned. Pass both to MLJ's interface functions to inspect it, e.g. MLJModelInterface.fitted_params(t.model, t.fitresult).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TrainedModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.SeriesState","page":"Home","text":"SeriesState\n\nEverything forecast needs about one series: its id (nothing for a single-series forecaster), the training target history that lag and rolling features are computed from, the first and last training timestamps, and the number of training rows. z_history is the history after the spec's TargetTransform, which lag and rolling features and the Fourier step index use, and transform_state is what untransform needs; without a transform, z_history is y_history. step0 is the Fourier step index of the first z_history value: 0 for a single series, and in a panel the steps from the earliest series' first value.\n\nRead them off a fitted panel forecaster via f.series; for a single-series forecaster the same values are reachable directly as f.y_history, f.t_last, f.t_start and f.n_train.\n\nExample\n\nf = fit(panel_spec, panel)\nfor st in f.series\n    println(st.id, \": \", st.n_train, \" rows ending \", st.t_last)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.SeriesState"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.nseries","page":"Home","text":"nseries(f::FittedForecaster) -> Int\n\nThe number of series the forecaster was fitted on: 1 for a single series, or the number of usable series in the panel. Series too short for the feature set are skipped at fit time, so this can be smaller than the number of ids in the input.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nf = fit(Forecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq=Day(1), id=:unique_id),\n        panel)\nnseries(f)                       # e.g. 3\n[s.id for s in f.series]         # the ids, in first-appearance order\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.nseries"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.fit","page":"Home","text":"fit(fc::Forecaster, data) -> FittedForecaster\n\nFit the forecaster on data, any Tables.jl-compatible table containing the spec's time and target columns (plus any exogenous columns, and the id column for a panel). For a single series the time column must be sorted, duplicate-free, and gap-free with respect to fc.freq. For a panel, rows may come in any order: fit sorts each series by time, and each series must be duplicate-free and gap-free.\n\nfit is a function owned by MachineLearningForecast (not an extension of MLJBase.fit or StatsAPI.fit); qualify as MachineLearningForecast.fit when another fit is in scope.\n\nExample\n\nfitted = fit(fc, df)\nfcast  = forecast(fitted, 28)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.fit"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.forecast","page":"Home","text":"forecast(f::FittedForecaster, h::Int; new_data=nothing) -> NamedTuple\n\nForecast h steps past the end of the training data. Returns a columntable (NamedTuple of vectors) with the spec's time column (the future timestamps t_last + freq, ..., t_last + h*freq) and :y_hat (the point forecasts). Being a columntable, the result is Tables.jl-compatible — pass it to DataFrame, CSV.write, etc. as-is.\n\nIf the feature set contains any Exogenous feature, new_data is required: a table with the time column and every exogenous column, covering all h future timestamps (extra rows are ignored). Without exogenous features, new_data must be omitted (it is ignored with a warning otherwise).\n\nFor a panel forecaster (one with id set), the result also has the id column, before the time column: h rows per series, each series forecast from its own last training timestamp. new_data must then carry the id column too and cover every series at each of its h future timestamps.\n\nExample\n\nfcast = forecast(fitted, 28)\nfcast = forecast(fitted, 28; new_data=future_exog)   # with Exogenous features\nfcast.y_hat                                          # the point forecasts\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.forecast"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Recursive","page":"Home","text":"Recursive()\n\nOne model; forecasts are fed back as pseudo-history to compute lag features. The model is trained once on the one-step-ahead frame and iterated over the horizon, so any horizon can be forecast — at the price of compounding prediction error through the fed-back history.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), strategy=Recursive(), freq=Day(1))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Recursive"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Direct","page":"Home","text":"Direct(max_horizon)\n\nOne model per horizon step 1..max_horizon. max_horizon fixes the number of models at fit time; forecasting beyond it errors (refit with a larger horizon or use Recursive).\n\nv1 simplification: at forecast time, the direct models condition their target-history features on the training-end history, while exogenous and time features vary per step.\n\nModel i predicts the target i steps ahead. At fit time its target-history features (lags, rolling statistics, differences, custom) come from the feature row j, and its time and exogenous features come from the target row j+i-1, which is what forecast feeds at step i. Taking both from row j would be a train/serve skew that silently degrades every step after the first.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), strategy=Direct(28), freq=Day(1))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Direct"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.ForecastStrategy","page":"Home","text":"ForecastStrategy\n\nAbstract supertype of forecasting strategies. Shipped strategies: Recursive and Direct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.ForecastStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.FeatureSet","page":"Home","text":"FeatureSet(features::AbstractFeature...)\nFeatureSet(features::AbstractVector{<:AbstractFeature})\n\nAn ordered collection of features. Order matters: it fixes the column order of the design matrix fed to the model. Output column names must be unique across the whole set.\n\nExample\n\nFeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), RollingMean(7), Calendar(:dayofweek), Fourier(365.25, 3))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.FeatureSet"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.outputnames","page":"Home","text":"outputnames(fs::FeatureSet) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nAll output column names produced by the set, in stable order (feature order, then each feature's own column order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.outputnames"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.minhistory","page":"Home","text":"minhistory(fs::FeatureSet) -> Int\n\nThe number of leading rows for which at least one feature is undefined; these rows are dropped from the training frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.minhistory"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.inputcolumns","page":"Home","text":"inputcolumns(f) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe user columns the feature f reads. forecast and backtest pick exactly these columns from new_data (or from the held-out rows). The default is Symbol[], Exogenous returns its columns, and an ExogenousFeature of your own must define it, otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown. Any feature may define it.\n\nExample\n\ninputcolumns(Exogenous(:promo, :price))   # [:promo, :price]\ninputcolumns(Lag(1))                      # Symbol[]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.inputcolumns"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.targetcolumnmask","page":"Home","text":"targetcolumnmask(fs::FeatureSet) -> Vector{Bool}\n\nMask over outputnames(fs): true where the column is produced by a TargetFeature (so it describes the target's own history and is anchored to the feature row), false for time/exogenous columns (which are anchored to the target row). Direct fitting needs this to shift the two groups independently — see src/direct.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.targetcolumnmask"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Lag","page":"Home","text":"Lag(k)\n\nLagged target y_{t-k} as feature column y_lag_k. Requires k ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nLag(7)   # the target seven steps ago, column :y_lag_7\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Lag"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingMean","page":"Home","text":"RollingMean(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollmean_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingMean(7)        # mean of the 7 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingMean"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingStd","page":"Home","text":"RollingStd(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollstd_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 2 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingStd(28)        # std of the 28 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingStd"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingMin","page":"Home","text":"RollingMin(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollmin_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingMin(14)        # min of the 14 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingMin"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingMax","page":"Home","text":"RollingMax(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollmax_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingMax(14)        # max of the 14 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingMax"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Diff","page":"Home","text":"Diff(k; lag=1)\n\nLagged difference of the target, y_{t-lag} - y_{t-lag-k}, as column y_diff_<k>_lag_<lag>. Requires k ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nDiff(1)       # first difference of yesterday: y_{t-1} - y_{t-2}\nDiff(7)       # week-over-week change as of yesterday: y_{t-1} - y_{t-8}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Diff"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Calendar","page":"Home","text":"Calendar(parts::Symbol...)\n\nCalendar features extracted from the time column, one column per part. Valid parts: :year, :quarter, :month, :weekofyear, :dayofweek, :dayofmonth, :dayofyear, :hour, :minute.\n\nExample\n\nCalendar(:dayofweek, :month)   # columns :dayofweek and :month\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Calendar"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Fourier","page":"Home","text":"Fourier(period, order)\n\nFourier seasonal terms sin(2πkn/period), cos(2πkn/period) for k = 1:order, where n is an integer step index (0-based from the first training timestamp, incremented by 1 per freq step). The index continues seamlessly into the forecast horizon, so there is no phase jump at the train/forecast boundary. In a panel, n counts from the earliest series' first training timestamp, so every series has the same Fourier values on the same date.\n\nperiod is expressed in units of freq steps, e.g. 365.25 for annual seasonality with daily data. order K produces 2K columns (fourier_<period>_sin_1..K, then fourier_<period>_cos_1..K).\n\nExample\n\nFourier(365.25, 3)   # annual seasonality, 3 harmonics, for daily data\nFourier(7, 2)        # weekly seasonality, 2 harmonics, for daily data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Fourier"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Exogenous","page":"Home","text":"Exogenous(cols::Symbol...)\n\nPass-through of user-supplied covariate columns that are known in the future (e.g. promotions, prices, holidays). At forecast time these columns must be supplied for the full horizon via the new_data keyword; in backtest they are sliced from the held-out data automatically. In v1, exogenous columns must be numeric (Real or Bool).\n\nExample\n\nExogenous(:promo, :price)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Exogenous"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.CustomFeature","page":"Home","text":"CustomFeature(name, f, minhistory)\n\nA user-defined target-history feature. f is a function of the target history vector up to and excluding the current row, returning one scalar (the value of the feature for that row). minhistory is the minimum number of history values f needs; rows with less history get missing during training and are dropped.\n\nDuring recursive forecasting, f is called on the growing history vector (actuals followed by earlier predictions), so it sees exactly the same kind of input as during training.\n\nExample\n\nusing Statistics\n# mean of the entire history so far (needs at least 1 observation)\nCustomFeature(:hist_mean, mean, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.CustomFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.AbstractFeature","page":"Home","text":"AbstractFeature\n\nRoot of the feature hierarchy. Concrete features are one of:\n\nTargetFeature: computed from the target's history (updated during recursive forecasting), e.g. Lag, RollingMean.\nTimeFeature: computed from the timestamp only (always computable for future rows), e.g. Calendar, Fourier.\nExogenousFeature: passed through from user data (must be provided for the future via new_data), i.e. Exogenous.\n\nEvery concrete feature implements four methods:\n\noutputnames(f): the names of the columns it produces, a Vector{Symbol}.\nminhistory(f): the number of history rows it needs before it is defined.\nmaterialize!(out, f, y, t, data): pushes its outputnames(f) columns for every training row onto out, with missing where it is undefined.\nfeaturevalues(f, y_hist, t_next, exog_row): a tuple of its values for one future row, used by recursive and direct forecasting.\n\nA feature that reads user columns also declares them through inputcolumns (an ExogenousFeature must).\n\nThe batch and single-row methods compute the same function. Every TargetFeature uses only information strictly before the current row's target, so rolling and difference features require lag ≥ 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.AbstractFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TargetFeature","page":"Home","text":"Features computed from the target's history (must be updated during recursion).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TargetFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TimeFeature","page":"Home","text":"Features computed from the timestamp only (always computable for future rows).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TimeFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.ExogenousFeature","page":"Home","text":"Features passed through from user data (must be provided for the future).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.ExogenousFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TargetTransform","page":"Home","text":"TargetTransform\n\nRoot of the per-series target transforms. A transform maps one series' training target y to the series z that the model learns and forecasts, and maps the forecasts of z back. Its state is fitted on that series' own training history only, so it never sees the forecast window, and backtest refits it in every fold.\n\nA transform implements two methods:\n\nfit_transform(t, y)    # -> (z, state); z may be shorter, aligned to y's end\nuntransform(t, ẑ, state)   # -> the forecasts of y for the forecasts ẑ of z\n\nFeatures are computed on z, and the first length(y) - length(z) training rows are dropped. Pass a transform as Forecaster(...; transform=...).\n\nExample\n\nfc = Forecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag.(1:12)...), freq=Month(1),\n                transform=TransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12),\n                                         LogRatio(12)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TargetTransform"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.fit_transform","page":"Home","text":"fit_transform(t::TargetTransform, y::AbstractVector{Float64}) -> (z, state)\n\nFit transform t on one series' training target y and return the transformed series z (possibly shorter, aligned to the end of y) and the state that untransform needs. Part of the TargetTransform contract.\n\nExample\n\nz, state = fit_transform(LastScale(3), [2.0, 4.0, 6.0, 8.0])   # z = y ./ 6\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.fit_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.untransform","page":"Home","text":"untransform(t::TargetTransform, ẑ::AbstractVector{Float64}, state) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nMap the forecasts ẑ of the transformed series back to the original scale, step 1 first. Part of the TargetTransform contract.\n\nExample\n\nz, state = fit_transform(LastScale(3), [2.0, 4.0, 6.0, 8.0])\nuntransform(LastScale(3), [1.5, 1.5], state)   # [9.0, 9.0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.untransform"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.NoTransform","page":"Home","text":"NoTransform()\n\nThe identity transform, and the default: the model learns the target as given.\n\nExample\n\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq=Day(1), transform=NoTransform())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.NoTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.MeanScale","page":"Home","text":"MeanScale()\n\nDivide each series by the mean of its whole training history, and multiply the forecasts back. Puts series of different levels on one scale for a global model.\n\nExample\n\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq=Day(1), id=:unique_id,\n           transform=MeanScale())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.MeanScale"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.LastScale","page":"Home","text":"LastScale(k)\n\nDivide each series by the mean of its last k training values (fewer if the series is shorter), and multiply the forecasts back. Unlike MeanScale it scales by the recent level, so a trending series starts its forecast near 1, inside the range a tree model has seen. Requires k ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nLastScale(12)   # monthly data: scale by the last year's mean\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.LastScale"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Deseasonalize","page":"Home","text":"Deseasonalize(period)\n\nClassical multiplicative seasonal adjustment, applied only to series that pass the M4 competition's seasonality test: the autocorrelation at lag period must exceed its 90% bound, which needs at least 3 * period values. A seasonal series is divided by seasonal indices: centred moving-average ratios averaged by position in the season and normalised to mean 1. The forecasts are multiplied by the index of their own position. Needs positive values when a series is adjusted, and period ≥ 2.\n\nExample\n\nDeseasonalize(12)   # monthly data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Deseasonalize"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.LogRatio","page":"Home","text":"LogRatio(window)\n\nModel each value as the log of its ratio to the mean of the window values before it, z_t = log(x_t / mean(x[t-window:t-1])), which is scale-free and keeps trending series stationary. The first window values have no ratio and are dropped. Forecasts are rebuilt step by step from the last window training values. Needs positive values and window ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nLogRatio(12)   # monthly data: ratio to the previous year's mean\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.LogRatio"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Difference","page":"Home","text":"Difference(lag)\n\nModel each value's change from lag steps before, z_t = y_t - y_{t-lag}, and rebuild forecasts by adding the changes back. Difference(1) removes a trend, Difference(12) a monthly season. The first lag values are dropped. Requires lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nTransformChain(Difference(1), Difference(12))   # mlforecast's Differences([1, 12])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Difference"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TransformChain","page":"Home","text":"TransformChain(transforms...)\n\nApply several transforms in order, each to the output of the one before, and undo them in reverse order. Needs at least one transform.\n\nExample\n\nTransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12), LogRatio(12))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TransformChain"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.backtest","page":"Home","text":"backtest(fc::Forecaster, data; horizon, initial=nothing, windows=nothing,\n         step=horizon, metrics=(mae, rmse)) -> BacktestResult\n\nExpanding-window time-series cross-validation. Pass exactly one of:\n\ninitial: train on rows 1:initial, forecast horizon steps, evaluate against the held-out actuals; advance the origin by step rows and repeat while a full horizon of actuals remains.\nwindows: hold out the last windows folds, step apart, the last ending at the end of the data. For a single series this is initial chosen so the final fold ends at the last row.\n\nFor a panel forecaster (one with id set), initial cuts folds on the global timestamp grid, since rows are spread across series: initial, step and horizon count distinct timestamps. Each fold trains on every row at or before the origin timestamp, forecasts each series forward from its own last observed timestamp, and scores the result by joining forecasts to actuals on (id, time), so ragged series contribute wherever they have actuals. windows instead counts the folds back from each series' own end, as the M competitions hold out each series' last values: in fold k, every series trains on its rows up to its own cutoff, and one global model is fitted on all of them. When the series span different calendar periods, a series' cutoff can fall before other series' training rows, as with every global model evaluated this way. The folds table then carries the id column too.\n\nmetrics is a tuple of callables (y, ŷ) -> Float64 (e.g. mae, rmse, smape). Scaled metrics that also need the fold's training target — mase, or your own, declared via needs_ytrain — are called as metric(y, ŷ; y_train=...) with that fold's training window supplied automatically. On a panel, such a metric is computed per series, with each series' own training values, and averaged with each series weighted by its scored points, so a scale never mixes series; other metrics pool the fold's scored rows. When the feature set includes Exogenous columns, they are sliced from the held-out data automatically (they are \"known\" in a backtest).\n\nExample\n\nresults = backtest(fc, df; horizon=28, initial=730, step=28,\n                   metrics=(mae, rmse, smape))\nresults.metrics   # per-fold and overall scores\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.backtest"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.BacktestResult","page":"Home","text":"BacktestResult\n\nResult of backtest. Both fields are columntables (NamedTuples of vectors, Tables.jl-compatible):\n\nfolds: one row per fold × horizon step, with columns :fold, :origin (the last training timestamp the row's forecast starts from), :step (1..horizon), the spec's time column, :y (actual), and :y_hat (forecast). A panel backtest adds the id column after :step.\nmetrics: one row per fold × metric (columns :fold, :origin, :metric, :value), plus one overall summary row per metric with fold=0 (:value is the mean of the per-fold values, :origin is missing). A fold's :origin is missing when its series start their forecasts at different timestamps.\n\nExample\n\nresults = backtest(fc, df; horizon=28, initial=730)\nresults.folds.y_hat   # every forecast, fold by fold\nresults.metrics       # per-fold and overall scores\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.BacktestResult"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.mae","page":"Home","text":"mae(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nMean absolute error, mean(|y - ŷ|).\n\nExample\n\nmae([1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0])   # 1.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.mae"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.rmse","page":"Home","text":"rmse(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nRoot mean squared error, sqrt(mean((y - ŷ)²)).\n\nExample\n\nrmse([1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0])   # ≈ 1.581\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.rmse"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.mape","page":"Home","text":"mape(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nMean absolute percentage error, mean(|y - ŷ| / |y|), as a fraction (0.10 = 10%). If any y is zero, MAPE is undefined: a warning is emitted and Inf is returned — prefer smape or mase for data with zeros.\n\nExample\n\nmape([10.0, 20.0], [11.0, 18.0])   # 0.1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.mape"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.smape","page":"Home","text":"smape(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nSymmetric mean absolute percentage error, mean(2|y - ŷ| / (|y| + |ŷ|)), as a fraction in [0, 2]. A term with y = ŷ = 0 contributes 0.\n\nExample\n\nsmape([10.0, 20.0], [11.0, 18.0])   # ≈ 0.1003\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.smape"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.mase","page":"Home","text":"mase(y, ŷ; y_train, m=1) -> Float64\n\nMean absolute scaled error: mae(y, ŷ) divided by the in-sample MAE of the seasonal-naive forecast with seasonality m on y_train (mean(|y_train[t] - y_train[t-m]|)). Values below 1 beat the naive baseline. If the naive error is zero (constant training series), a warning is emitted and Inf is returned.\n\nExample\n\nmase([10.0, 12.0], [11.0, 11.0]; y_train=[1.0, 3.0, 1.0, 3.0])   # 0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.mase"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.needs_ytrain","page":"Home","text":"needs_ytrain(metric) -> Bool\n\nTrait declaring that metric needs the fold's training target in addition to the actuals and forecasts. backtest calls such metrics as metric(y, ŷ; y_train=...) and ordinary ones as metric(y, ŷ). On a panel it calls them once per series, with that series' own training values.\n\nDefaults to false; true for mase. Define it for your own scaled metrics so they can be passed to backtest / tune:\n\nmy_mase(y, ŷ; y_train) = mae(y, ŷ) / mae(y_train[2:end], y_train[1:end-1])\nMachineLearningForecast.needs_ytrain(::typeof(my_mase)) = true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.needs_ytrain"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.tune","page":"Home","text":"tune(fc::Forecaster, data; grid=nothing, tuner=GridSearch(), max_evals=nothing,\n     horizon, initial=nothing, windows=nothing, step=horizon,\n     metric=smape) -> TuneResult\n\nTune the whole forecasting pipeline on backtest score. Each candidate is a NamedTuple of Forecaster field overrides (e.g. model, features, strategy); the candidate spec is rebuilt from fc with those overrides, backtested with backtest(spec, data; horizon, initial, step, metrics=(metric,)), or with windows in place of initial, and scored by the mean of metric across folds (lower is better). Pass exactly one of initial and windows; windows counts each series' folds back from its own end, which lets tune work on panels whose series span different calendar periods.\n\ngrid: NamedTuple mapping Forecaster field names to candidate value lists. Required for the batch strategies (GridSearch, the Cartesian product; RandomSearch, n uniform draws). A sequential user-defined TuningStrategy owns its own space and may ignore it. Before any fit, tune throws an ArgumentError if a batch candidate it will evaluate has a :strategy that is not a strategy, such as :recursive.\nmax_evals: optional evaluation budget; the loop stops after this many candidates even if the strategy proposes more.\nCandidate failures never abort the search: the error is caught, the score recorded as missing (and reported to the strategy via tell!), the message stored in the result table's :error column, and the candidate excluded from ranking. A non-finite mean score (NaN or Inf) counts as a failure. If every candidate fails, tune throws an ErrorException listing the distinct error messages.\n\nReturns a TuneResult with the score table, the best spec, and the best spec refit on all of data. Ties are broken by first-seen order.\n\nExample\n\nusing EvoTrees\nresult = tune(fc, df;\n    grid = (model    = [EvoTreeRegressor(eta=0.05), EvoTreeRegressor(eta=0.1)],\n            features = [FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7)),\n                        FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), Fourier(365.25, 3))]),\n    horizon=28, initial=730, step=28, metric=smape)\nresult.best_fitted   # ready to forecast\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.tune"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TuneResult","page":"Home","text":"TuneResult\n\nResult of tune:\n\ntable: a columntable (NamedTuple of vectors) with one row per evaluated candidate — the overridden parameters (one column per key), :mean_score and :std_score (the mean and standard deviation of the metric across backtest folds; std_score is missing with a single fold), and :error (the error message if the candidate failed, missing otherwise). Failed candidates are excluded from ranking.\nbest::Forecaster: the best candidate spec (NOT fitted).\nbest_fitted::FittedForecaster: the best spec refit on ALL of the data.\n\nExample\n\nresult = tune(fc, df; grid=(strategy=[Recursive(), Direct(28)],),\n              horizon=28, initial=730)\nresult.table.mean_score             # one score per candidate\nresult.best                         # the winning spec\nforecast(result.best_fitted, 28)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TuneResult"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TuningStrategy","page":"Home","text":"TuningStrategy\n\nAbstract supertype of tuning search strategies for tune. Shipped strategies: GridSearch and RandomSearch.\n\ntune is an ask/tell loop: it asks the strategy for the next candidate, scores it with backtest and tells the strategy the score. The batch strategies serve a precomputed candidate list through this loop; a sequential strategy, such as a Bayesian optimizer defined outside this package, drives it directly.\n\nExtending with your own (sequential) strategy\n\nSubtype TuningStrategy and implement two methods; tune will drive them in an ask/tell loop:\n\nask(s::MyStrategy) -> Union{NamedTuple,Nothing} — return the next candidate: a NamedTuple of Forecaster field overrides (keys among :model, :features, :strategy, :freq, :target, :time, :id, :transform), drawn from your search space. Return nothing to signal termination.\ntell!(s::MyStrategy, candidate, score) — receive the result of evaluating candidate: a Float64 backtest score (lower is better), or missing if the candidate failed to evaluate.\n\nTermination is signalled either by ask returning nothing, or by the max_evals budget enforced by tune. Your strategy owns its search-space representation (construct it with whatever space description it needs).\n\nExample\n\nmutable struct FirstK <: TuningStrategy\n    candidates::Vector{NamedTuple}\n    i::Int\nend\nMachineLearningForecast.ask(s::FirstK) = s.i > length(s.candidates) ? nothing :\n                                         (c = s.candidates[s.i]; s.i += 1; c)\nMachineLearningForecast.tell!(s::FirstK, candidate, score) = nothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TuningStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.GridSearch","page":"Home","text":"GridSearch()\n\nExhaustive search over the Cartesian product of the grid values passed to tune. Deterministic candidate order (first grid key varies fastest); ties in score are broken by first-seen order.\n\nExample\n\ntune(fc, df; grid=(model=[m1, m2], strategy=[Recursive(), Direct(28)]),\n     tuner=GridSearch(), horizon=28, initial=730)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.GridSearch"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RandomSearch","page":"Home","text":"RandomSearch(n; rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nRandom search: n candidates drawn from the grid passed to tune, sampling each grid key independently and uniformly (with replacement, so duplicates are possible). Pass an explicit rng for reproducibility.\n\nExample\n\nusing Random\ntune(fc, df; grid=(model=models, features=featuresets),\n     tuner=RandomSearch(10; rng=Xoshiro(1)), horizon=28, initial=730)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RandomSearch"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.ask","page":"Home","text":"ask(strategy) -> Union{NamedTuple, Nothing}\n\nAsk a TuningStrategy for its next candidate: a NamedTuple of Forecaster field overrides (keys among :model, :features, :strategy, :freq, :target, :time, :id, :transform). Return nothing to signal that the search is finished. Part of the public tuning-extension API — implement it for your own strategy subtypes; see TuningStrategy for the full contract.\n\nExample\n\nmutable struct FirstK <: TuningStrategy\n    candidates::Vector{NamedTuple}\n    i::Int\nend\nMachineLearningForecast.ask(s::FirstK) = s.i > length(s.candidates) ? nothing :\n                                         (c = s.candidates[s.i]; s.i += 1; c)\n\ns = FirstK([(strategy=Recursive(),), (strategy=Direct(28),)], 1)\nask(s)   # (strategy = Recursive(),)\nask(s)   # (strategy = Direct(28),)\nask(s)   # nothing: the search is finished\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.ask"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.tell!","page":"Home","text":"tell!(strategy, candidate, score)\n\nReport an evaluation result back to a TuningStrategy: candidate is the NamedTuple previously returned by ask, and score is the Float64 mean backtest metric (lower is better) or missing if the candidate failed to evaluate. Part of the public tuning-extension API — implement it for your own strategy subtypes; see TuningStrategy for the full contract.\n\nExample\n\nmutable struct Logged <: TuningStrategy\n    candidates::Vector{NamedTuple}\n    scores::Vector{Union{Missing,Float64}}\nend\nMachineLearningForecast.ask(s::Logged) = length(s.scores) < length(s.candidates) ?\n                                         s.candidates[length(s.scores) + 1] : nothing\nMachineLearningForecast.tell!(s::Logged, candidate, score) = push!(s.scores, score)\n\ns = Logged([(strategy=Recursive(),), (strategy=Direct(28),)], [])\ntune(fc, df; tuner=s, horizon=28, initial=730)\ns.scores   # each candidate's mean backtest score, or missing if it failed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.tell!"}]
}
